{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgyey3k7u8dN3rd85S6i2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The flag keeps the old pre-1.0 function names working. With `--legacy-imports`, `scripts/build.py` adds `pkg/_legacy.py` to the bundle. That file re-exports the current functions under their old names:\n\n- `group_totals` as `sum_by`\n- `parse_amount` as `to_decimal`\n\nSo any code that still imports those names from `pkg._legacy` keeps working. Without the flag, that file isn't built and those imports fail.\n\n**Is it cruft?** Probably, but nothing in the repo can confirm it:\n- Nothing in the repo uses `_legacy`, `sum_by` or `to_decimal`. Not the code, the tests, the README or the changelog.\n- No reason is recorded anywhere. The flag first appears in the \"Release 1.3.2\" commit, the earliest point the history goes back to, with no explanation. The runbook gives reasons for other steps (step 2, for example) but not this one.\n- The real question is whether any users outside this repo still import the old names, and the repo can't answer that.\n\nThe 1.4.0 bundle I just built includes the shim, the same as earlier releases, so nothing changed for users.\n\nIf you want to remove it, the safe way is to treat it as a deprecation, because some users may still depend on it. Check whatever usage data you have, or announce the removal in the changelog first. Then drop the flag and the shim in a later release, and update step 4 of the runbook to match. I can draft that change if you want, but I'd leave it out of the 1.4.0 release."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":269,"cache_read_input_tokens":20213,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":269},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,